spinDeg` turns it, both eased with the section's own curve (the same shared easing as its motion, the camera pan and the camera path) and applied **per frame from elapsed time** rather than at creation, so a late join starts mid-spin instead of at the still frame. A spin **accumulates** across motion cycles (3 × 120° ends at 360°, no snap back), a stretched image's spin adds to its bearing so a spinning bolt still flies along its own line, and the animation does not rotate a mask (the region lives in the parent's space). A filter's own **strength animates** too: `filterTo` carries it from `filter.strength` to that end on the section's own curve, and with `repeats` it **pulses per cycle** instead of accumulating (a strength is a value, not a bearing — the answer scale already gives); both ends are clamped to the kind's own range (a blur cannot animate to 0: dropping the filter is how an author says that), switching the kind **drops** the animation (16 is a heavy blur and an impossible grayscale), an emptied box removes the key, and a late join starts mid-animation. `inspect` reports the strength read back out of the live filter — a colour matrix stores the author's number in one coefficient — rather than a remembered plan. A visual can also be **masked**: one of the shared crosshair's own shapes (circle/cone/ray/rect — `point` is refused because it has no area) measured in scene units against the scene's grid, resolved by the host into a polygon of offsets from the anchor, so the mask travels with a followed effect and a stored cue replays the same polygon. `invert` cuts the region out instead of confining to it; a broken square/hex grid metric is refused rather than silently read 1:1, and each shape accepts only its own fields. A region can also be **drawn by hand** (D-315): `mask.kind: "polygon"` with 3–64 `points` in scene units from the anchor, in order — validated as the shape it is (each point exactly `{x, y}` within ±5 000 units, a region with an area, no self