, so a region that would be refused at save time is refused here in the host's words with the draft untouched; the overlay opens **seeded from the mask the author already has** (a polygon seeds a circle), so drawing is also adjusting; a cancelled pick leaves the draft as it was; the author's `walls`/`invert` switches survive the gesture and a polygon's `points`/`scaleTo` do not. A followed section gives something up, and says so in the pick's hint beforehand: drawing sets a **point** anchor, which cannot also follow a token, so the follow is cleared by the same rule the anchor controls use rather than left for the host to refuse at save. The region itself **animates**: `mask.lengthTo` grows it (traveling as a unit-free ratio of the host-resolved polygon, so a recipient still never learns what "15 ft" is in pixels, and scaling as a whole — a rectangle's width grows with its depth; a hand-drawn polygon, which has no length, grows by its own `scaleTo` ratio instead, D-315) and `mask.spinDeg` turns it about the anchor, on the section's own easing; the two reuse the transform's own rules exactly (a turn **accumulates** across cycles, a size **restarts**, pinned by one shared cycle helper). Its **cross axis animates on its own** too (D-314): `widthTo` is the width a ray/rect widens to — perpendicular to its own axis, so the reach it covers is untouched — and `spreadTo` is the aperture a cone opens to, its points keeping their radius; each is offered only where it means something (a circle has no cross axis, a ray has no aperture) and bounded by its own numbers, and a wall-bounded region refuses both with the same baked-trim sentence. The cross axis travels as a **ratio plus the frame it lives in** (`axisDeg`), so a recipient still learns no metric; one bit of shape (`fan`) decides how it is drawn — a ray/rect stretches across that axis, a cone swings its fan about the apex at the same radius, because a sideways stretch would fatten the arc into an ellipse instead of opening the cone — and beside a pinned width a `lengthTo` growth becomes the along axis alone, so "grow to 60, widen to 40" lands each number where it was written. A circle takes no turn because it has no facing, an emptied box removes the key, a still region is still drawn exactly once into the *same* graphics an animated one is redrawn into, and `inspect` reports the drawn polygon's `radius`, its own `bounds` and — honestly — `bearingDeg`, which is `null` for a circle and a rectangle because neither polygon says which way it faces. A region can also be **bounded by walls**: `mask.walls` trims the resolved polygon against the scene's own sight segments — the exact list the fog's vision worker is fed, so a window never trims, a closed door does, an open one does not and a locked one does — by taking the **smaller of the two radial extents** at every angle where either polygon bends (including where their edges cross, so a switch between the mask's boundary and a wall's is not chorded), which is why the region stops *at* the wall rather than near it; the trim is computed in the region's own offsets (the polygon keeps travelling with a followed visual) and considers only blockers within the mask's own reach. Because it is baked, a wall-bounded region **cannot animate** — a growth or turn is refused by name and the wizard clears an already-entered one and puts the fields away — and two degenerate anchors are refused rather than drawn: one standing on a wall, and a trim that leaves no area. **Who a cue is for is authored, and it is four forms** (D-316, SQ-18's "named recipients"): `scene`, `gm`, `caller`, or **`{ players: [...] }`** — 1–32 user ids in the author's order, no repeats (a repeated user is refused, not folded, so "who is in this list" has one answer), empty refused because an audience of none is a cue with no purpose. The same vocabulary at the run level and on a camera section, resolved by **one** rule (`fxAudienceAllows`) that the host's four decisions all call: the preflight fan-out (where a caller-side narrowing can still only narrow), the post-commit re-check that re-reads the macro's *current* audience, the stored-instance check (the record's own audience **and** the timeline's must include the viewer), and the request gate — which is now a tightening rather than a new rule, because publishing a cue was never a licence to fire it *at other people*: a player may invoke only a timeline that includes them. Ids are the world's users, so a cue addressed to an offline player — or to one who has not joined yet — is still addressed to them, and existence is the *wizard's* guarantee (it offers the world's own list) rather than something every save re-proves. `gm` remains the only audience that also hides the **document**; the audience decides delivery, and the host is what keeps the cue from everyone else. A **camera section is also targetable** by the same four forms, and the host builds the cue **per recipient**, so a viewer outside the audience receives the run *without* that section (a bystander cannot infer a GM-only camera from the socket), while a viewer left with no sections receives no cue at all. **The audience never travels**: the field is stripped from every delivered section, because a recipient of a chosen-players camera would otherwise read the whole list out of their own payload — the membership query SQ-18 keeps out of socket traffic, one hop in — and a chosen list does not silently include the GM, who receives nothing from it either. In the wizard, both audience selects gain "Chosen players…" with a checklist of the world's users (name + the world's word for the role), seeding the authoring user so the draft is never an empty list the host would refuse; ticking appends in the author's order and unticking removes one id, so a list naming a user this client cannot see survives an edit of the others. The authoring-time fitness warning reads a chosen list as a *player* audience unless every user in it is GM-side. **The table also answers for itself**: `fx.media` (0x4e, client → host) is one viewer's word about one asset of a cue it was sent — `ready` (in hand, with what the fetch cost), `late` (in hand, past the section's start), `failed` (`fetch` or `decode`) or `unsupported` (this browser cannot decode the format, said before a byte is fetched or while decoding) — and the host folds those answers into a **second** `fx.delivery` for the same run: counts per asset, the worst asset named by the requester's **own section index** (no asset or user identifier, and a closed reason set instead of a detail string that could carry a URL or a hash), `spoke`/`complete` so the line knows whether it is an answer or a snapshot, and the slowest successful fetch. The answer is **the latest, not the worst**: a fetch that failed at cue start and succeeded when the section needed the bytes has the media, so the later ack replaces it and the client only speaks when its answer changes. The first line goes out at the earlier of a failure (the GM can still stop a cue that is playing wrong), a complete answer, or the run's own media span plus two seconds (never under four, never over a minute, one unref'd sweep timer); exactly **one correction** may follow, and only when the whole answer changed — including a viewer that was silent and has since spoken, because a line still claiming "have not reported yet" after everybody reported would be worse than a late one. A refused format is not even downloaded (`canPlayType` answering `""` is a hard no; a shell without a DOM fetches as usual), a local mute or a zero channel is not reported at all (a device preference stays on the device), a `lateMedia: "skip"` still reports `late` because the bytes *were* there, a draft preview never acks, and a persistent instance or a reviewed-script cue gets no receipt — a loop has no single moment whose answer means anything. Receipts are bounded (32 runs, oldest evicted), expectations are fixed when the cue is fanned out (a session that joined later has no standing to answer), and anything unexpected is ignored rather than answered, so a guessed run id learns nothing. Only a GM/assistant requester reads it, for the same reason a player-initiated request gets no preflight line. **A sound now has a place** (D-309): `at` (any anchor, frozen at emit like every other one — a sound does not chase a token) with `radius` in scene units (1–1 000, required with `at`) and two switches, `pan` and `muffle`; each of the three is refused *by name* without a position rather than quietly ignored, `at` resolves through the same `anchor()` rule (a point, or a source/target token's current centre) and `radius` becomes host-resolved `radiusPx` on the scene's own grid metric, so a recipient never re-derives "60 ft" against a metric the host did not check. A sound with no position stays exactly what it was: the author's volume, for everyone, everywhere. Distance is a straight line — full at the source, silent at the rim, linear between, the *predictable* falloff an author can check against the map — measured on the client from its own listener (its own token, else the centre of its own view) and **re-measured on a 100 ms tick while the cue plays**, so a walking listener hears the sound approach; panning is `clamp(dx/r, ±1)` in screen space and only when the author asked. Occlusion is the host's alone, because a client is never handed walls: a `muffle` sound is tested **per recipient** against the scene's **sound** axis plus door state (`soundSegments`), from that recipient's own token at ownership level 3 — a token *assigned* to them, not one the table's default merely lets them move — with the answer baked into that recipient's copy as `occluded: true` (no listening-point coordinates ever travel) and a viewer with nothing of their own on the scene sent **no** answer at all, since the host cannot honestly name their camera. Pan and muffle need Web Audio, so they go through one lazily-created `AudioContext` per page (`source → low-pass → stereo panner`, the element's own `volume` still carrying the level so the mix and the fades keep composing); a device without it plays the cue at its distance level, shows what it is *actually* playing, and reports the reduction in its own delivery line as `reduced` / `spatial-unavailable`. Emptying a placement deletes all four fields instead of writing `undefined` (the D-295 msgpack trap), and the wizard's "Place on map…" pick is **point-only** because a sound is at a place and has no area. **A look can also be kept on its own** (D-310, SQ-12's last clause): a preset is a macro document of a new kind (`fxPreset`) whose whole payload is the draft's sections — no `persistent` and no *timeline-level* `audience` (a camera section's own audience travels with the section, because it is part of the look) — and loading one replaces the draft's sections under **fresh ids**, so the same preset can be loaded twice into one timeline while the draft keeps whatever lifecycle the GM had already chosen; nothing about a preset reaches the table until the resulting timeline is saved and run, so a preset cannot become a second, weaker path to playing an effect. Each section is validated by the *sequence* validator itself and a failure blames the section that caused it ("preset section 2 (fx-two): …"), the bundle holds **1–8 sections**, and a mixed document is refused by name in both directions (a `sequence`/`script`/`scriptState`/`summon` on a preset, a `preset` on a runnable macro). Presets are authoring state: GM/assistant-only to create, edit and delete (a player's attempt is `forbidden`), never projected to players, and the media a preset references counts as *referenced* — so the D-306 entitlement rules gain a case instead of changing meaning. The wizard puts a name box and "Save draft as preset" under the saved timelines, with **Load / Update from draft / Rename / Delete** per row: saving needs no timeline first, and deleting a preset leaves the timelines built from it untouched. Its browser spec authors a text-plus-sound draft, saves it as a preset from the name box alone (no timeline needed), clears the draft and loads it back with both sections intact under **new** ids, edits the draft, pushes it onto the preset with "Update from draft", renames it and proves the edit reached the **world** by clearing the draft and loading what the host now holds, then saves the loaded draft as a timeline and runs it for real (the cue renders and the preset's own sound reaches the device list), and finally deletes the preset and shows the timeline built from it untouched. Unit coverage is 8 new cases: 5 in `tests/core/fxPresets.test.ts` (each section re-validated by the *sequence* validator including the D-309 positional-sound rule, a failure naming the offending section by index and id, the version/1–8/unknown-field shape, fresh ids with a duplicate-proof mint, and the 1–64-character name plus both directions of the mixed-document refusal), 1 in `tests/core/assetAccess.test.ts` (a preset's media is *referenced* — not loose art, and still not a player's), 1 in `tests/core/projection.test.ts` (a world-readable preset is still authoring state: absent from a player's snapshot and from direct ops, present for the GM and assistant) and 1 in `tests/host/sync.test.ts` (create/rename reaching the store, the preset refused by the FX path as an unknown timeline, a forged preset-and-sequence document rejected by name, an empty bundle rejected, a player's create/update/delete all `forbidden`, and the GM's delete undoable). `pnpm test` **3 775 passed / 12 skipped** (297 of 299 files), `pnpm typecheck` **63 components / 0 blocking / 1 advisory**, `pnpm lint` exit 0, `pnpm build` → **3 855 399 B raw / 1 105 491 B gzip** (inside the 6 MB budget), and the `fx_sequence` + `summons` Chromium batch is **66/66 with `--repeat-each=2`** after the e2e WAV fixture was replaced (`wavSilence()` writes a real ~96 KB PCM file: the old header-only fixture is accepted by Chromium and then ends immediately, which made every "play a sound, then read the device list" assertion a race the suite lost on a loaded machine; the animation specs' frame-count bars are likewise stated as samples-across-the-span with the growth asserted against the authored reach). Only camera sections take the field; image/text/sound/wait sections refuse it as unknown. The handover is explicit: a real drag/wheel/zoom-button cancels the claim *without* writing a camera (the viewer's gesture wins in the same frame, and the rest of that run's camera track is ignored) while a stop restores the pre-cue view exactly. The wizard's **Pick on map…** now goes through a **shared SQ-10 crosshair** (also used by the summon window): point/circle/cone/ray/rect, cell/hex-centre snap plus 15° direction snapping, an outline the author is shown (and which is checked, so a circle half-buried in a wall is refused), range/LOS/reach constraints drawn from the consumer's own host rules, a live fault list in the host's own order, Esc/cancel that leaves the draft untouched, and committed placements that can be **named and reused** by a later pick (names are panel state — the sequence receives plain points). Both of SQ-12's placement modes now exist: the click above, and **drag source → target** (`Drag source → target…`), where one press-and-drag fills a section's start *and* destination, the drag's own bearing (derived and 15°-snapped, overridable with the rotate buttons) becomes the shape's facing, and its measured length is reported in scene units — a drag checks **both ends** plus the segment between them (`line-blocked`, so a wall across the drag is refused), names the end each fault belongs to ("Start: …" / "End: …"), samples the shape at the source only, places **nothing** if the drag never leaves its starting cell, and leaves the line on screen after the pointer lifts so a name or a nudge can still be added before the one commit button writes it. `summonPlacementError` and the preview now call the same segment rule, pinned by a test that asserts they agree, and the wizard still **previews an unsaved draft locally** — same validation and renderer, but no host request, world op, instance or recipient, and a persistent draft previews one pass. New `fx.list`/`fx.stopMatching` supplies limited wildcard-name and exact origin/token ID matching with owner-scoped, undoable stop, and the GM Live FX manager now offers a separate scene-local, host-revalidated atomic stop by wildcard name or exact sequence/source/target ID (up to 16; stop requests from players are denied). A GM-reviewed Worker now has `fx.playAndWait` and a bounded `fx.sequence()` composition of sequential/parallel host-approved FX, finite waits, callbacks and reviewed nested-macro calls (≤32 sections, ≤8 parallel jobs, 7-second wait budget/finite cue deadline within a 10-second Worker). A production Chromium fixture authors a real saved cue and executes a reviewed sequence chain. **Not** a general Sequencer scheduler: no persistent await, client decode/finish acknowledgement, arbitrary nested sequences, transactional multi-section rollback, cancellation graph or extension API. **Delivery (SQ-13/A10)** is now partly closed: media a timeline will need is prefetched ahead of its cue according to each viewer's own 0–8000 ms window (`0` = lazy fetch at cue time), readiness is measured *before* the fetch (so a slow client is identified instead of hidden), a section more than 120 ms late either starts when its bytes land or is skipped according to that viewer's setting, and the run produces one report line — nothing at all when it arrived on time. A per-device panel (GM Settings and the player's guide) adds reduced camera motion (a pan cuts to its destination, a shake is skipped) and FX-sound muting that never affect anyone else; the OS `prefers-reduced-motion` switch only seeds a default a viewer can override. The host reports a partial audience to a GM requester via `fx.delivery` (`0x4d`, counts only: audience/rights/anchor/media, plus how many recipients received the run **without** a section targeted elsewhere and how many entitled viewers were left with none of it — a withheld section is not a skip), and the wizard warns before a save about registry gaps (asset absent, codec this browser refuses, GM-only media in a scene-audience timeline). **Sound lifecycle (SQ-09/SQ-17)** is now partly closed: a sound section names a **channel** (`sfx`/`music`/`ambience`/`voice`, host-validated as a closed set) and may carry **fades** bounded by its own duration; each device mixes those channels with its own gains (master mute plus four faders), the player re-evaluates gain on a short tick so a fade ramps and a fader moved mid-cue reaches the element, a silenced channel is **skipped without fetching its bytes** (the same `muted` reason as the master switch), a loop fades in once and holds, and the device panel lists what *this browser* is playing with a "Stop here" that leaves the host's instance untouched. Still required: the full chain/programmatic API, persistent lifecycle, the crosshair's shapes-committed-*as*-regions mode (a drag is offered for a visual section's start/destination, not for a camera look-at, a path waypoint or a summon) and the remaining on-canvas player modes, the camera's remaining shapes (targeting is by the audience — named users now exist (D-316), but there are no **groups**, no per-*token* targeting, a visual/sound section still reaches every recipient, and the delivery notice counts rather than naming who was targeted — plus per-leg durations and curved/looping legs), masks/elevation, positional/occluded audio and a dedicated host sound manager, effect masks (blend modes, one bounded filter or a 2–4 entry chain (D-313), host-resolved circle/cone/ray/rect masks with cutouts and hand-drawn polygon regions now exist; drawing a region **on the canvas** with the crosshair, a wall-bound polygon that is not star-shaped about its anchor, holes/rings inside a region, animation of tint/alpha, keyframes or multi-stop tracks, and masks on sound/camera/wait sections do not), extensible sections and measured synchronization/performance. Positional audio (D-309) covers distance, screen-space pan and host-answered muffling against the **sound** axis; it does not model elevation, HRTF, room reverb, a ray-counted falloff, a tunable cutoff, or walls that open *mid-cue* (occlusion is baked per recipient at emit; the level keeps following the listener). FX **presets** (D-310) cover SQ-12's save/load/edit/delete clause as *the look*: they hold no run or live instance, cannot nest, are not shared between worlds or through any UI beyond the world file itself, and have no branch in the Live FX manager. **Item binding (D-311, extended by D-312)** closes SQ-12's last clause and A09's stretch: `MacroDocument.fxItem` (`{actorId, itemId, onFailureId?, recognition?: auto|success|failure, enabled?, events?}`) lives on the **timeline**, so the existing macro projection decides discovery — a reader who cannot read the timeline sees no binding, and the item window's line is computed by the same lookup the use path fires, so it can never describe a cue the cast would not ask for. The bound cue is an ordinary `fx.request` through `prepareFx`, so rights, audience, token visibility and the 300 ms lead are re-checked by the host and a binding **grants nothing** (a player's own `requestSequence` for the bound timeline is still refused as unpublished); the cue is requested **after** the caller's flow commits (charges, slots, hit points), which is what makes "the branch uses the committed result" true — a refused use plays nothing, a committed success plays the bound timeline, and a committed **failure** (a lost spell, a held/missed touch delivery, spell resistance, a made save) plays `onFailureId` or answers `null` (nothing) when no failure cue is bound, while a multi-round cast that has not landed is `unknown